  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 117 AM EDT Sun Jul 26 2026\n\nThe pattern for the last week of July can be characterized by \ncontinual broad ridging to the west and troughing to the northeast, \nplacing us within the region of northerly to northwesterly flow. The \nnear stationary boundary from the past few days still resides to our \nsouth and will continue to drive the weather, at least through \ntoday. Through this morning and possibly into the afternoon, streams \nof showers and storms will glide over the region from the northwest. \nIf they are able to line up and train over the same area, isolated \nflooding could be possible. Later today tries to dry out from \nnorthwest to southeast.\n\nLater tonight and into tomorrow morning still remains a big question \nmark. 24 hours ago, models depicted a fairly stout shortwave \nrounding the top of the ridge, potentially bringing an MCS over or \nclose to the area. It could very well just miss us to the west. \nSomething to watch, even though high-res models cant's seem to all \nagree. Later in the day tomorrow may be dry for most with some \nisolated activity around.\n\nA potentially stronger shortwave that may pass over the Great Lakes \ninto Tuesday, will eventually bring a cold front across the region \nTuesday afternoon and evening. This has now moved up in time \ncompared to Friday night's forecast. Given the adjustment, assuming \nit doesn't change, the potential for severe weather will need to be \nclosely monitored. At the time of this discussion, Day 3 and 4 from \nthe SPC highlight northeastern parts of the area.\n\nFollowing Tuesday potentially having the hottest temperatures of the \nweek (upper 90s to low 100s heat indices in the valley), the cold \nfront will send temperatures down a few degrees Wednesday until the \nend of the forecast period. Later Wednesday into Friday could be a \nbrief dry period with lower humidity before we turn the page to \nAugust next weekend where precipitation chances return once again.",
